.TH COFFER 1 "2026-09-16" "coffer 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
coffer \- growable, user-mountable encrypted single-file containers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B coffer
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.br
.B coffer create
.I file
.RB [ \-\-max\-size
.IR SIZE ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-command
.IR CMD ]
.RB [ \-\-save
.I ALIAS
.B \-\-mountpoint
.IR DIR ]
.br
.B coffer mount
.RI [ file | alias
.RI [ mountpoint ]]
.RB [ \-\-save
.IR ALIAS ]
.RB [ \-\-foreground ]
.RB [ \-\-idle\-timeout
.IR DURATION ]
.RB [ \-\-compact\-on\-idle
.IR DURATION ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-command
.IR CMD ]
.RB [ \-\-read\-only ]
.RB [ \-\-log
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B coffer umount
.RI [ mountpoint | alias ]
.br
.B coffer add
.I alias file mountpoint
.RB [ \-\-idle\-timeout
.IR DURATION ]
.RB [ \-\-compact\-on\-idle
.IR DURATION ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-command
.IR CMD ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-read\-only ]
.br
.B coffer remove
.I alias
.br
.B coffer list
.br
.B coffer check
.IR file | alias
.RB [ \-\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-command
.IR CMD ]
.br
.B coffer backup
.IR file | alias
.I dest
.RB [ \-\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-command
.IR CMD ]
.br
.B coffer passwd
.IR file | alias
.RB [ \-\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-command
.IR CMD ]
.RB [ \-\-new\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B coffer info
.IR file | alias
.RB [ \-\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-command
.IR CMD ]
.br
.B coffer compact
.IR file | alias
.RB [ \-\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-command
.IR CMD ]
.br
.B coffer completions
.I shell
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B coffer
creates a single-file encrypted container that any user can create and
mount without root or sudo. Directories and files are rows in a
.BR SQLCipher (3)
(encrypted SQLite) database; file content is stored in 128\ KiB chunks.
Mounting is done via
.BR FUSE (4),
so a mount is owned by, and only accessible to, the user who created it.
.B coffer \-\-version
prints the package version together with the exact git commit it was
built from, so two installations claiming the same version number can
still be told apart.
.PP
The container has no fixed size: SQLite simply extends the backing file
as data is written, unless a ceiling is set at creation time with
.BR \-\-max\-size .
Once mounted, the container behaves like an ordinary directory \-
.BR cp (1),
.BR mv (1),
.BR rsync (1)
and file managers all work normally against it.
.PP
Every command that opens an existing container (all except
.BR create )
prompts for the container password on the controlling terminal, with
input masked. If standard input is not a terminal (piped or scripted
use), the password is instead read as a single plain-text line from
stdin. The password is never accepted as a command-line argument, so it
cannot leak via
.BR ps (1)
output or shell history. Every such command also accepts
.B \-\-password\-file
.I FILE
as an explicit alternative to prompting \- reads one line from
.IR FILE ,
useful for cron jobs and systemd units. A world\- or group\-readable
password file prints a warning but is not refused outright.
.B \-\-password\-command
.I CMD
runs
.I CMD
through
.B sh \-c
and takes the first line of its standard output as the password, so any
secret store with a command-line client can supply it without the
password ever touching a file or a command line:
.BR "secret-tool lookup coffer work" " (GNOME Keyring, KDE Wallet),"
.BR "pass show coffer/work" ,
.BR "op read op://Private/coffer/password" " (1Password),"
.BR "bw get password coffer" " (Bitwarden),"
or a graphical prompt such as
.BR "zenity \-\-password" .
The command's stdin is closed, so one that would wait for input fails
instead of hanging; its stderr passes through. A failing or silent
command is an error. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-password\-file .
Stored with
.B add
or
.BR \-\-save ,
either becomes the alias's default; a
.BR \-\-password\-file " or " \-\-password\-command
on the command line replaces both stored values.
.PP
Wherever a command takes a container
.IR file ,
the alias of a vault registered in
.I ~/.coffer/config
(see
.BR add ", " remove ", " list
and
.B FILES
below) can be given instead.
.BR mount " and " umount
then take the mountpoint from the registry as well, and with no argument
at all mount the one registered vault (respectively: unmount the one
registered vault that is currently mounted), or offer a numbered menu
when there are several. An alias is a bare word; an argument containing a
.B /
or starting with
.B .
or
.B ~
is always a path and never looked up, and a bare word that matches no
alias is tried as a path.
.PP
.BR mount ", " passwd ", and " compact
take an exclusive lock on the container before doing anything else, so at
most one of them can run against a given container at a time \- attempting
a second one fails immediately with a clear error rather than racing two
writers.
.BR check ", " backup ", and " info
deliberately do not lock: WAL mode already gives them a safe, consistent
view alongside an active writer, which
.B backup
in particular depends on. This lock is only as reliable as
.BR flock (2)
is on the underlying filesystem \- solid locally, but historically
inconsistent over NFS \- so it should not be relied on as a hard guarantee
for a container shared over NFS from multiple machines.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "create " file " [\-\-max\-size " SIZE ]
Create a new container at
.IR file .
Prompts for the password twice (with confirmation). By default the
container grows until the host disk is full; pass
.B \-\-max\-size
.RI ( SIZE
is a plain byte count or a number suffixed with
.BR K / M / G / T ,
e.g.
.BR 10G )
to reject writes past a fixed ceiling with ENOSPC instead. With
.BR \-\-password\-file ,
the password is read once from the given file instead of prompted for
twice. With
.B \-\-save
.I ALIAS
(which requires
.B \-\-mountpoint
.IR DIR ),
the new container is also registered under that alias exactly as
.B add
would, so
.B coffer mount
.I ALIAS
works from then on.
.TP
.BI "mount [" file | alias " [" mountpoint "]] [\-\-save " ALIAS "] [\-\-foreground] [\-\-idle\-timeout " DURATION "] [\-\-compact\-on\-idle " DURATION "] [\-\-read\-only] [\-\-log " FILE ]
Mount
.I file
at
.I mountpoint
as the current user.
.I mountpoint
is created if missing and must be empty. If the first argument is the
alias of a registered vault (see
.BR add ),
its file is mounted at its registered mountpoint (or at
.I mountpoint
if one is given anyway), with the entry's stored options as the defaults
for any flag not given on the command line. With no arguments at all,
the one registered vault is mounted; if several are registered, a
numbered menu (alias, mountpoint, file, size on disk, last modified,
mounted or not) asks which one, or - without a terminal to ask on -
fails and says to name the alias.
.B \-\-save
.I ALIAS
first registers the file/mountpoint pair, plus the options given here,
under that alias, same as
.BR add .
By default
.B coffer
daemonizes and returns control to the shell immediately; pass
.B \-\-foreground
to keep it attached (useful for debugging \- errors and FUSE
diagnostics print to the terminal instead of being discarded).
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
.RI ( DURATION
is a plain second count or a number suffixed with
.BR s / m / h / d ,
e.g.
.BR 30m )
auto\-unmounts the container after it has seen no filesystem activity for
that long. Off by default. Refuses to run if
.I file
is already mounted (or a
.B passwd
/
.B compact
is in progress against it).
.B \-\-compact\-on\-idle
.RI ( DURATION
in the same format) periodically runs
.B VACUUM
once the mount has been idle that long, but only if there is a meaningful
amount of reclaimable space (at least 64\ MB and at least 10% of the file);
most idle periods reclaim nothing, so this is normally a no\-op. Unlike
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ,
it keeps running afterward instead of unmounting. It shares the same
connection the FUSE loop itself uses, and
.B coffer
dispatches FUSE requests from a single thread by default, so a
mid\-VACUUM
.B every
filesystem call blocks \- not just writes \- until it finishes, which on a
large container can be minutes. Off by default; prefer running
.B compact
manually while the mount is idle instead, for containers used
interactively.
.B \-\-read\-only
(short
.BR \-r ,
also
.BR \-\-ro )
mounts the container read\-only: the file is opened read\-only, the
kernel refuses every write with EROFS, and so does
.B coffer
itself. Handy for looking into a backup copy or an archive without any
chance of touching it. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-compact\-on\-idle .
The container is still locked against a second
.B mount
or a concurrent
.B passwd
/
.B compact
for as long as it is mounted.
.B \-\-log
.I FILE
(short
.BR \-l )
appends the mount's own messages \- mounted, unmounted, idle unmount,
auto\-compact, and any error from those \- to
.IR FILE ,
each line timestamped. A daemonized mount otherwise has nowhere to print
them (its output goes to
.IR /dev/null ).
The file is created with mode 0600. Stored with
.B \-\-save
or
.BR add ,
it becomes the alias's default.
.TP
.BI "umount [" mountpoint | alias ]
Unmount a container previously mounted at
.IR mountpoint ,
or at the registered mountpoint of
.IR alias .
With no argument, unmounts the one registered vault that is currently
mounted, or asks which via the same menu as
.B mount
if several are. Shells out to
.BR fusermount3 (1)
(falling back to
.B fusermount
or
.BR umount (8)
if unavailable). If the plain unmount fails (e.g. "device or resource
busy" from a stale connection after a crashed mount daemon, or another
process - possibly another user's desktop session - still holding the
mount), automatically retries with a lazy unmount before giving up - no
.BR sudo (8)
needed for this. After a lazy detach it also aborts the FUSE connection, so
the mount daemon exits and releases the container's lock right away instead
of lingering until the last holder lets go; anything that still had files
open there gets I/O errors from then on.
.TP
.BI "add " alias " " file " " mountpoint " [\-\-idle\-timeout " DURATION "] [\-\-compact\-on\-idle " DURATION "] [\-\-password\-file " FILE "] [\-\-password\-command " CMD "] [\-\-log\-file " FILE "] [\-\-read\-only]
Register
.I file
(which must already exist) and
.I mountpoint
(which needn't) under
.I alias
in
.IR ~/.coffer/config ,
replacing an existing entry of that name. Both paths are stored absolute.
The options are stored with the entry and become the defaults for
.B mount
.IR alias ;
.B \-\-read\-only
makes every mount of the alias read\-only (there is no flag to override
a stored read\-only back to writable).
An alias consists of letters, digits,
.BR \- ", " _ " and " . ,
and must start with a letter or digit.
.TP
.BI "remove " alias
Forget
.IR alias .
The container file itself is left untouched.
.TP
.B list
Print every registered vault: alias, mountpoint, file, size on disk, last
modified, and whether it is mounted right now.
.TP
.BI "check " file | alias
Verify container integrity without modifying it: SQLCipher's per-page
HMAC check followed by SQLite's structural
.B integrity_check
(authoritative for containers past 4\ GB \- see
.B BUGS
below). Exits non-zero if either check finds a real problem. Safe to run
while
.I file
is mounted.
.TP
.BI "backup " file | alias " " dest
Write a byte-consistent copy of
.I file
to
.I dest
using SQLite's online backup API. Safe to run while
.I file
is mounted and being actively written to.
.TP
.BI "passwd " file | alias
Change the container password. Prompts for the current password, then
the new password twice (with confirmation), unless
.B \-\-password\-file
/
.B \-\-new\-password\-file
are given. Refuses to run while
.I file
is mounted.
.TP
.BI "info " file | alias
Print on-disk size, logical data used, the configured size ceiling (if
any), and directory/file/symlink counts. Safe to run while
.I file
is mounted.
.TP
.BI "compact " file | alias
Run
.B VACUUM
against the container to reclaim disk space freed by earlier deletions
(SQLite does not shrink the file on its own). May temporarily need up to
roughly twice the container's current size in free disk space while it
runs. Refuses to run while
.I file
is mounted.
.TP
.BI "completions " shell
Print a shell completion script for
.I shell
.RI ( bash ", " zsh ", " fish ", " elvish ", or " powershell )
to stdout. bash/zsh/fish completions are installed automatically by this
package and by
.BR "make install" ;
this is only needed manually for a
.B "PREFIX=$HOME/.local"
source install.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success.
.B coffer check
exits
.B 1
if it finds a genuine HMAC failure or a failing structural integrity
check. Other commands exit non-zero on any error (wrong password,
mountpoint not empty, I/O failure, etc.), with a message on stderr.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COFFER_CONFIG
Path of the vault registry to use instead of
.IR ~/.coffer/config .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.coffer/config
The per-user vault registry: one
.BI [ alias ]
section per registered vault, with
.BR file " and " mountpoint
lines and optionally
.BR idle_timeout ", " compact_on_idle ", " password_file ", " password_command ", " log_file " and " read_only
(same formats as the corresponding flags;
.B read_only
takes true or false). Written atomically with mode
0600 (a freshly created
.I ~/.coffer
gets 0700). Safe to edit by hand: comments on their own line are kept,
and a leading
.B ~
in a path means
.BR $HOME .
.TP
.I vault.coffer
The container itself: a single SQLCipher (encrypted SQLite) database
file.
.TP
.IR vault.coffer \-wal ", " vault.coffer \-shm
SQLite's Write-Ahead Log and shared-memory index, present alongside the
container while it is open (mounted, or mid-command). Do not delete
these while the container is in use; a clean unmount checkpoints and
removes them.
.SH SECURITY
No root or sudo is required to create or mount a container. Unix
permission bits stored inside a container are recorded and reported but
.B not
enforced: whoever supplies the correct password gets full read/write
access to everything inside. This matches a personal-container use case
(comparable to VeraCrypt), not a multi-user shared filesystem. The FUSE
mount itself is only accessible to the mounting user by default.
.SH BUGS
SQLCipher has a confirmed upstream bug where
.B "PRAGMA cipher_integrity_check"
misreports every page past the 4\ GB mark as HMAC-failed on containers
larger than that, independent of this project (reproduced across three
separate SQLCipher builds).
.B coffer check
detects this specific false-positive pattern and treats the structural
.B integrity_check
(which actually decrypts and verifies every page) as authoritative, so
it will not report a large, healthy container as corrupt \- while still
correctly failing on real corruption anywhere in the file.
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation, source, and the container's on-disk format:
.br
.UR https://github.com/rtulke/cofferFS
.UE
